From: PT1500-H10 port <pt1500@local>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: apply device-tree LED default-state and re-apply after reset

phylib registers PHY LEDs with led_classdev_register_ext() passing only the
fwnode and the device name.  The LED core exports
led_init_default_state_get() as a helper for drivers but never applies
"default-state" itself, so a PHY LED node carrying "default-state = on" is
registered with brightness 0 and stays dark for its whole lifetime.

That is what kept the 2.5G port LED of the PT1500-H10 dark: the RTL8221B
hisgmii driver also skips its VEND2 LED_CFG programming whenever the device
tree declares LED nodes (it checks list_empty(&phydev->leds)), so neither path
ever wrote the LED registers.

Apply the requested state at registration time through the LED core, which
keeps led_cdev::brightness - and therefore sysfs - truthful, then expose
phy_leds_reapply() so a driver can restore the state after a PHY reset.
rtl8221b_hisgmii_config_init() calls it because its enable() path issues
BMCR_RESET, which clears the VEND2 LED registers that were programmed moments
earlier by phy_probe() -> of_phy_leds().

Verified on hardware: with no userspace LED configuration the three PHY LEDs
come up lit (brightness=1, trigger=none) and indicate link/activity in
hardware; configuring them via OpenWrt's /etc/config/led switches them to the
netdev trigger as expected.

--- a/include/linux/phy.h
+++ b/include/linux/phy.h
@@ -921,6 +921,7 @@
 };
 
 #define to_phy_led(d) container_of(d, struct phy_led, led_cdev)
+void phy_leds_reapply(struct phy_device *phydev);
 
 /**
  * struct phy_driver - Driver structure for a particular PHY type
--- a/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/phy_device.c
@@ -3426,11 +3426,47 @@
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
+	/*
+	 * "default-state" is a DM property the LED core only exposes through
+	 * led_init_default_state_get(); it is never applied by
+	 * led_classdev_register_ext() itself.  Without this the LED stays at
+	 * brightness 0 for its whole lifetime even though the firmware asked
+	 * for it to be on.
+	 */
+	if (led_init_default_state_get(of_fwnode_handle(led)) ==
+	    LEDS_DEFSTATE_ON)
+		led_set_brightness_sync(cdev, LED_ON);
+
 	list_add(&phyled->list, &phydev->leds);
 
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/**
+ * phy_leds_reapply - push the current LED state back to the PHY hardware
+ * @phydev: target PHY
+ *
+ * A PHY reset (for example BMCR_RESET) clears the vendor LED registers, which
+ * silently turns the port LEDs off even though their sysfs brightness is still
+ * 1.  Drivers that reset the PHY in config_init() must call this afterwards so
+ * the requested state is programmed again.
+ *
+ * Must not be called with phydev->lock held: the LED callback takes it.
+ */
+void phy_leds_reapply(struct phy_device *phydev)
+{
+	struct phy_led *phyled;
+
+	list_for_each_entry(phyled, &phydev->leds, list) {
+		if (!phyled->led_cdev.brightness_set_blocking)
+			continue;
+
+		phyled->led_cdev.brightness_set_blocking(&phyled->led_cdev,
+							 phyled->led_cdev.brightness);
+	}
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(phy_leds_reapply);
+
 static int of_phy_leds(struct phy_device *phydev)
 {
 	struct device_node *node = phydev->mdio.dev.of_node;
--- a/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_hisgmii.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_hisgmii.c
@@ -354,6 +354,10 @@
 	if (ret < 0)
 		phydev_warn(phydev, "HiSGMII enable failed: %d\n", ret);
 
+	/* BMCR_RESET above clears the VEND2 LED registers, so the state the LED
+	 * class devices already hold must be programmed again.
+	 */
+	phy_leds_reapply(phydev);
 	__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_SGMII, phydev->possible_interfaces);
 	__set_bit(PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_2500BASEX, phydev->possible_interfaces);
 	return 0;
